Description: Learns the structure of a Bayesian network (a directed acyclic
    graph) from continuous data with the dynamic programming search of
    CausNet (Sharma and Millstein (2023) <doi:10.1186/s12859-023-05159-6>),
    in which the parents of each variable are chosen from a screened set of
    candidate parents. Candidate parents are screened either with Pearson
    correlation tests or with stability selection (Meinshausen and Buhlmann
    (2010; preprint <doi:10.48550/arXiv.0809.2932>)), where the number of
    variables selected per subsample is derived from a per-node bound on the
    per-family error rate (PFER): the expected number of falsely selected
    candidate parents of each variable (node), each screened as its own
    family of the other variables. Summed over the screened nodes, the
    expected total number of falsely selected candidate parents (not edges
    of the final network) is at most the number of screened nodes times
    this bound. The search can be restricted to the neighbourhood of an
    outcome variable (phenotype-driven search), and variables that cannot
    be part of any edge are removed before the search.
    Parent sets are scored with the Bayesian information criterion (BIC) of
    linear regression models (default), the BGe score from 'bnlearn', or a
    user-supplied score function.
